<resources>
    <!--
      V2-37: the *window* theme — what the platform paints before the first
      composition exists. It used to be light-only with a hardcoded
      `windowLightStatusBar=true`, so launching in dark mode showed a bright
      window and dark-on-dark status bar icons until Compose took over.

      Two halves to the fix, and both are needed:

       - This theme, overridden in values-night. It runs before any of our code
         does, so it can only follow the *system* night setting — which is the
         best available guess at launch, and is exactly right whenever the app's
         theme is SYSTEM (the default).
       - `SyncSystemBars` in the Compose tree, which corrects the bar icon
         appearance once the real scheme is known. That is what makes an
         explicit LIGHT/DARK/AMOLED app setting agree with the system bars even
         when it disagrees with the system.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Messages" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:colorPrimary">@color/brand_primary</item>
        <item name="android:colorAccent">@color/brand_primary</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/window_background</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
    </style>
</resources>
